<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for a Developer ID signed RichOS.app.

  NO DOUBLE HYPHEN MAY APPEAR ANYWHERE IN THIS COMMENT, and that is not a style
  note. XML forbids the two-hyphen sequence inside a comment, so a flag written
  out literally here makes the whole file malformed. It cost a signing run on
  2026-09-01: this comment said "sign developer-id" with the flag's two leading
  hyphens on its line 8, and `codesign` refused the file outright with
  "Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 8".
  The trap is that `plutil -lint` reports this file OK — Apple's lenient plist
  parser skips the malformed comment and AMFI's strict one does not — so the
  obvious check passes on a file that cannot sign. `xmllint` and Python's expat
  both name it correctly ("not well-formed, line 8, column 5"), and
  `app/scripts/signing-setup.test.sh` case F10 now signs a throwaway binary with
  this file so a regression is caught by the thing that actually consumes it.
  Refer to the signing mode in prose, as this paragraph does.

  APPLIED ON ONE PATH ONLY. `app/scripts/package-app.sh` passes this file to the
  bundler as a config overlay when, and only when, it is asked for the
  developer-id signing mode. It is referenced nowhere in `tauri.conf.json`, so an
  ordinary `cargo tauri build` and the ad-hoc packaging path never see it. Whether
  to enroll with Apple at all was CEO decision 1.1 in
  richos-hq/wiki/ceo-decisions.md, and it is closed: the membership exists, and a
  Developer ID Application certificate has existed on this machine since
  2026-09-01.

  WHY THE ONE ENTITLEMENT. `app/src-tauri/Info.plist` carries
  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, which is what macOS shows the user in the prompt.
  Under the hardened runtime that string is not sufficient on its own: the process
  also needs `com.apple.security.device.audio-input`, or CoreAudio hands it an
  endless stream of zeros with nothing in any log. A notarized hardened-runtime app
  needs BOTH — the requirement flagged in Info.plist's own comment and in
  richos-hq/wiki/packaging-and-signing.md, "What RichOS specifically needs" item 2.

  WHY NOTHING ELSE. Every added entitlement widens what the app is permitted to do
  and what Apple scrutinizes at notarization, so this list stays at what is
  demonstrably needed rather than what might be. Deliberately absent:

    - Accessibility (paste-at-cursor, the global hotkey) is a TCC grant obtained
      through AXIsProcessTrusted. There is no entitlement for it and adding one
      would be cargo cult.
    - JIT (`com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit`) belongs to WKWebView's own
      out-of-process content process, which carries its own entitlements from
      Apple's framework. The host app does not need it.
    - Library validation stays ON. It is only in the way once unsigned third-party
      dylibs get loaded, which RichOS does not do.

  WHAT WILL NEED REVISITING, named now rather than discovered later. When the
  sidecars land — `whisper.cpp`, `ffmpeg` — each must be signed with the same
  identity and sealed into the bundle. (`claude-agent-acp` and a bundled Node
  runtime were on this list until 2026-08-31; `wiki/ceo-decisions.md` §16 deleted
  both, and the `claude` binary is the CUSTOMER's install, outside our bundle and
  outside our signature — which is the point, and a licence condition besides.)
  If any of them cannot be (a prebuilt binary that resists re-signing), the escape
  hatch is `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`, which should be
  argued for explicitly at that point and not pre-added here.

  WHAT IS VERIFIED NOW, and what is still not. As of 2026-09-01 a Developer ID
  Application identity exists on this machine (BF4D68E6…, team TZ33A4QCZJ) and
  this file HAS been used to sign: two RichOS.app builds carry it, the signed
  entitlements read back out of the signature with codesign contain
  com.apple.security.device.audio-input, the hardened runtime is on, and both
  builds produced the identifier-and-team designated requirement the certificate
  was bought for. STILL UNVERIFIED: that this set notarizes (no notarization run
  has been made), and that voice mode works under the hardened runtime with a
  granted microphone (that is a human sitting in front of the app, not a
  property any file holds).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
